<title>USA: U.S. Jan leading indicators +0.3 pct-Conference Bd.</title>
<headline>U.S. Jan leading indicators +0.3 pct-Conference Bd.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-03-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Conference Board index of leading, coincident and lagging indicators (1992=100) and percent changes from previous months, seasonally adjusted.</p>
<p>    -			Index---Jan   Dec    Nov     Oct</p>
<p> Leading Indicators    103.0   0.3   0.1    0.1R   Unch</p>
<p> Coincident Indicators 114.1   0.1   0.3R   0.4    0.2R</p>
<p> Lagging Indicators    104.6  -0.2   0.1R  Unch   -0.2R</p>
<p>Following are components of the index of leading indicators with net contributions to monthly changes.</p>
<p>    -			    Jan    Dec    Nov    Oct</p>
<p> Average Workweek	  -0.10   0.10   Unch   Unch</p>
<p> Initial Jobless Claims   0.11  -0.07  -0.01   Unch</p>
<p> Consumer Goods Orders    0.15  -0.05  -0.03   0.02</p>
<p> Slower Deliveries	 -0.11   Unch   0.03   0.02</p>
<p>    -			    Jan    Dec    Nov    Oct</p>
<p> Capital Goods Orders     0.04   Unch  -0.06   Unch</p>
<p> Building Permits	  -0.02   Unch   0.04  -0.03</p>
<p> Stock Prices		 0.06   0.02   0.10   0.08</p>
<p> Money Supply		 0.06   0.09   0.07   Unch</p>
<p> Treasury Yield Curve     0.06   0.02  -0.08  -0.05</p>
<p> Consumer Expectations   -0.01  -0.03   0.05   Unch</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had expected January leading indicators to rise 0.5 percent.</p>
</text>
